Careful: dlsym(RTLD_NEXT, ...) inside a constructor may recurse if dlsym itself calls pthread functions. It's generally OK.

Note: using a constructor is important so the interposition is set up before the game's libs load. But actually, since we define the functions in the preloaded lib, the dynamic linker binds the game's PLT entries to our definitions directly (no constructor needed) — our functions are the definitions; we call the real ones via dlsym(RTLD_NEXT,...). The dlsym lookup happens lazily on first call. Good.

But careful: `dlsym(RTLD_NEXT, "pthread_mutex_init")` from within our preloaded lib returns the next definition after ours in the search order → libc's ✓.

